<div class="wrap">
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">Life Saving Victoria · First Aid Reporting · 2025/26 Season</div>
    <h1>First aid form upgrade: 2025/26 season analysis</h1>
    <p class="lede">What the new first aid form recorded in its first season, what it made measurable for the first time, where it needs work, and what comes next. All figures are re-derived from the raw case records for six seasons (7,406 cases).</p>
  </header>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="kicker">Background</div>
    <h2>About the first aid form</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Whenever a lifesaver, lifeguard or event first aider treats a person, a first aid form is completed in LSV's incident management system. The record captures who was treated, what they presented with, what was done, and how the patient left care. Cases a crew classifies as major can be linked to an LSV Communications incident number so the first aid record and the operational incident sit together.</p>
      <p>Until November 2025 every case saw the same flat form: a fixed set of fields (injury type, initial treatment, body location, activity at the time, who the patient was released to) plus one free-text notes box where all other clinical detail had to be written. Data that did not fit the fixed fields existed only as prose.</p>
      <p>The new form presents question sets based on the case. The nature of the presentation (injury, medical, envenomation, environmental, mental health, suspected drowning, resuscitation, drug or alcohol) opens a specific-problem question set for that nature. Treatments given open their own sets: administering oxygen asks for method, time applied and duration; administering methoxyflurane asks for vial number, dose times, contraindications, adverse effects and pain ratings before, at two minutes and at four minutes; performing resuscitation asks for equipment, airway assessment, method, time of first compression, defibrillation time, CPR cycles, first AED rhythm, shock count and the patient's state at handover. The discharge set records the pathway, ambulance handover and the patient's condition, and a closing set asks whether the responder needs peer support or experienced occupational violence. A member sees only the questions that apply to the case in front of them.</p>
      <p>The old form was retired on 9 November 2025 and the new form went live on 12 November 2025. This analysis compares the 1,494 new-form cases against the five prior seasons and examines what the new data shows.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tiles">
      <div class="tile"><div class="v">87.1%</div><div class="l">of cases documented within one hour of treatment</div><div class="d">Not measurable under the old form</div></div>
      <div class="tile"><div class="v">~4 min</div><div class="l">median time from opening a form to its final edit</div><div class="d">95.0% of forms reach completed status</div></div>
      <div class="tile"><div class="v">96.6&ndash;100%</div><div class="l">answer rate on conditional question sets when presented</div><div class="d">Across all six clinical branches; gaps explained below</div></div>
      <div class="tile"><div class="v">14.0%</div><div class="l">of patients unidentified, down from 27.9% last season</div><div class="d">Unidentified patients halved</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

