        <div class="row"><div class="name">Medical</div><div class="track"><div class="bar" style="width:10.1%" title="14 of 138"></div></div><div class="val">10.1%</div></div>
        <div class="row"><div class="name">Nature (top level)</div><div class="track"><div class="bar" style="width:1.7%" title="24 of 1,443"></div></div><div class="val">1.7%</div></div>
        <div class="row"><div class="name">Injury</div><div class="track"><div class="bar" style="width:1.1%" title="11 of 1,011"></div></div><div class="val">1.1%</div></div>
        <p class="sub after">The injury and nature lists fit practice closely. The environmental list is missing common options (the free text is dominated by cold and heat exposure variants and dizziness), and the envenomation free text is mostly unidentified stings and insect stings. The change register already includes option-list and field changes.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="kicker">Outliers explained</div>
    <h2>What sits behind each exception</h2>
    <p class="note">Every figure above that is not 100 per cent, or that differs from what the headline suggests, is accounted for here from the structured fields of the records concerned. No narrative text is reproduced.</p>

    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Envenomation specific problem recorded on 96.6%: what are the other 3.4%?</div><div class="a">Seven cases. Two are forms still open in the workflow (the specific-problem step has not been reached). Five are completed forms where the member reached and passed the step without selecting a specific problem; all five received sting-typical treatment (ice or hot water), and two of the five are classified major with no referral and no ambulance. The workflow allows the specific-problem question to be skipped on completion; making it mandatory would close the gap.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Injury specific problem on 99.3%: the other 0.7%?</div><div class="a">Seven cases: four open forms (one with no treatment recorded at all) and three completed forms that skipped the step. One of the open forms is classified major. Environmental has one completed form that skipped the step (treatment recorded as "Other").</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Eleven of twelve suspected drownings are major. The twelfth?</div><div class="a">A member of the public aged 12, recorded as a non-fatal suspected drowning, conscious at discharge, treatment recorded as "Other", no referral, no ambulance called, no incident number and no narrative. The structured record describes a low-acuity episode that the crew did not escalate, which is why it was not classified major; the absence of any narrative or incident link for a suspected drowning is itself a consistency point for the action-form work.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Four of five mental health cases are major. The fifth?</div><div class="a">A panic attack in a 27-year-old, no treatment required, no referral. Classified minor consistently with those fields.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Five resuscitation-nature cases but seven resuscitation records?</div><div class="a">Two cardiac arrests were recorded under the medical nature, and the members completed the resuscitation question set for them. Counting by the question set, seven resuscitations were performed; counting by nature, five. The nature-level count undercounts and the question-set count is the reliable one.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Envenomation is 3.4% major. Which seven cases?</div><div class="a">Three stingray injuries (two to hospital with an adult, one by ambulance), one combined medical and envenomation case transported by ambulance, one jellyfish sting with no referral, and the two majors with no specific problem and no referral described above. The stingray cases drive the severity; the last three are classification inconsistencies rather than clinical escalations.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Forty-one cases documented a day or more after treatment. Who are they?</div><div class="a">Thirty-four are injuries and eleven are major. Nineteen come from club-event cohorts, and two volunteer clubs account for sixteen of the forty-one. Thirty-six of the forty-one were subsequently completed. The pattern is retrospective entry after club events at a small number of units, not a system-wide lag.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Three cases show treatment after the form was created.</div><div class="a">The recorded treatment time is 44 minutes, 10 hours and 9 days later than the form's creation. These are treatment-time entry errors (the time was entered in the future relative to when the form was opened) and were excluded from the timeliness segments.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Twenty-four completed forms have no nature recorded.</div><div class="a">All twenty-four have a free-text reason for presentation and a treatment, and the workflow marks all steps complete. The nature question can be passed without an answer; it should be mandatory, because every downstream question set keys off it.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">249 cases classified major but 253 linked to an incident number.</div><div class="a">The two flags are set independently: 22 major cases have no incident number and 26 incident-linked cases are not classified major, 48 records (3.2%) where the classification and the link disagree. This is the consistency problem the action form is designed to remove.</div></div>
    <div class="outlier"><div class="q">Fourteen methoxyflurane records but eight pain trajectories.</div><div class="a">Six of the fourteen have no starting pain score entered. Two of the eight scored cases start at 1 of 10; the remaining six start at 8 to 10 and fall by 0 to 6 points within four minutes.</div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="kicker">Training targets</div>
    <h2>Questions that are presented but not answered well</h2>
    <p class="note">Capture-rate gaps on questions that matter clinically. They corroborate the training flags already raised internally (full name and SAMPLE history) and add two more.</p>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="row"><div class="name">SAMPLE: medications</div><div class="track"><div class="bar" style="width:6%" title="6.0% of all cases; 8.8% of majors"></div></div><div class="val">6.0%</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="name">SAMPLE: medical history</div><div class="track"><div class="bar" style="width:7%" title="7.0% of all cases; 12.9% of majors"></div></div><div class="val">7.0%</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="name">SAMPLE: allergies</div><div class="track"><div class="bar" style="width:7.2%" title="7.2% of all cases; 9.6% of majors"></div></div><div class="val">7.2%</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="name">Ambulance case number</div><div class="track"><div class="bar" style="width:8.2%" title="18 of 220 hospital-pathway cases"></div></div><div class="val">8.2%</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="name">Outcome question, majors</div><div class="track"><div class="bar" style="width:28.9%" title="72 of 249 major cases"></div></div><div class="val">28.9%</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="name">Full name (not first only)</div><div class="track"><div class="bar" style="width:57.8%" title="FullName on 57.8% of cases; a further 28.2% first name only"></div></div><div class="val">57.8%</div></div>
      <p class="sub after">SAMPLE capture roughly doubles on major incidents (12.9 / 9.6 / 8.8 per cent) but remains low where it matters most. Date of birth, address and nationality declined when they became optional; whether to require them is a form-design decision rather than a member-performance issue.</p>
